00:02:24.795 --> 00:02:25.912
- [Voiceover] Mike asks,

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:27.725
"If you don't care about the competition,

00:02:27.725 --> 00:02:29.442
"what do you say when a client asks you

00:02:29.442 --> 00:02:30.558
"about a competitor,

00:02:30.558 --> 00:02:32.555
"and why you're better?"

00:02:32.555 --> 00:02:34.995
- Mike, this is a tremendous question.

00:02:34.995 --> 00:02:37.170
I've dealt with this in
the past on VaynerMedia's

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.353
road to success,

00:02:39.353 --> 00:02:40.903
and I still do all the time.

00:02:40.903 --> 00:02:42.201
People bring up

00:02:42.201 --> 00:02:44.154
competitors and I answer

00:02:44.154 --> 00:02:46.151
in a very condescending way towards them.

00:02:46.151 --> 00:02:49.726
I dislike my competitors
in the context of the game.

00:02:49.726 --> 00:02:51.770
Some of them are friends
outside of the game

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:52.875
and I can share a beer,

00:02:52.875 --> 00:02:53.805
I can separate it.

00:02:53.805 --> 00:02:55.570
I can share a beer at a game

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:56.961
or be cordial in public,

00:02:56.961 --> 00:02:59.378
but deep down, I'm not
a fan of my competitors.

00:02:59.378 --> 00:03:00.632
I dislike them.

00:03:00.632 --> 00:03:02.257
And when people bring them up,

00:03:02.257 --> 00:03:04.902
I tend to tear them down
as quickly as possible

00:03:04.902 --> 00:03:06.667
or remind them why I'm better

00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:08.571
and come up with historical things like,

00:03:08.571 --> 00:03:10.289
you know, in the agency world for example,

00:03:10.289 --> 00:03:11.081
I say,

00:03:11.081 --> 00:03:12.750
Oh you mean those people
that try to win awards

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.842
in a world where I grew
up trying to sell stuff?

00:03:14.842 --> 00:03:16.003
Anything that I think

00:03:16.003 --> 00:03:17.396
will make that person realize

00:03:17.396 --> 00:03:19.949
that it is a worthwhile
venture to go with us

00:03:19.949 --> 00:03:21.019
and why it's a

00:03:21.019 --> 00:03:23.617
wasteful conversation to
care about somebody else.

00:03:23.617 --> 00:03:24.873
That is something I will do.

00:03:24.873 --> 00:03:25.616
Now look,

00:03:25.616 --> 00:03:27.286
I'm a good salesman, a good talker,

00:03:27.286 --> 00:03:28.633
I've got great tact,

00:03:28.633 --> 00:03:30.722
I have enormous empathy and great feel.

00:03:30.722 --> 00:03:32.055
I can read the room.

00:03:32.055 --> 00:03:34.098
So I know where to navigate in real time,

00:03:34.098 --> 00:03:36.328
which is why I sell and win so often.

00:03:36.328 --> 00:03:37.953
You may not have that skill set

00:03:37.953 --> 00:03:39.023
and you may come across as

00:03:39.023 --> 00:03:42.040
you know, inappropriate, awkward,

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.340
you know,

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.781
inappropriate.

00:03:44.781 --> 00:03:46.266
And so, you know,

00:03:46.266 --> 00:03:47.891
for all of you on this side over here

00:03:47.891 --> 00:03:49.098
who can read the room,

00:03:49.098 --> 00:03:50.445
can move quickly on your feet,

00:03:50.445 --> 00:03:51.885
can rock and roll that way,

00:03:51.885 --> 00:03:54.069
just punch your competitor in the face.

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:55.230
Over here,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:56.343
not as smooth,

00:03:56.343 --> 00:03:57.504
you're not quite as sure,

00:03:57.504 --> 00:03:58.897
you don't understand
where they want to go,

00:03:58.897 --> 00:04:00.246
you've got to go with a different route.

00:04:00.246 --> 00:04:01.312
I don't know what that route is,

00:04:01.312 --> 00:04:02.884
but it's (bleep).

00:04:02.884 --> 00:04:04.279
- [Voiceover] Dan asks,

00:04:04.279 --> 00:04:05.347
"White lies.

00:04:05.347 --> 00:04:06.691
"Do you believe the hype?

00:04:06.691 --> 00:04:08.224
"It seems you can't win in business

00:04:08.224 --> 00:04:10.455
"without bending the truth."

00:04:10.455 --> 00:04:12.591
- Dan, this is a really intense question.

00:04:12.591 --> 00:04:14.123
I want you to show India right now,

00:04:14.123 --> 00:04:15.972
because I'm giving her props

00:04:15.972 --> 00:04:18.250
for navigating the
questions and finding stuff.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.034
This question is one

00:04:21.034 --> 00:04:23.124
that I'm really struggling to figure out

00:04:23.124 --> 00:04:24.240
what the right answer is,

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.887
which leads to the answer which is,

00:04:28.607 --> 00:04:31.438
appropriate embellishment

00:04:31.438 --> 00:04:33.712
feels appropriate at times to me,

00:04:33.712 --> 00:04:36.464
but I'm scared to say that out loud.

00:04:37.094 --> 00:04:39.091
If you feel like you're growing into it

00:04:39.091 --> 00:04:41.090
within weeks

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:43.503
or maybe maximum a couple of months,

00:04:43.503 --> 00:04:45.442
I think it's okay. Right?

00:04:45.442 --> 00:04:47.682
Like, I feel comfortable
saying VaynerMedia's

00:04:47.682 --> 00:04:49.261
a 450-person agency right now

00:04:49.261 --> 00:04:51.351
even though we're 417,

00:04:51.351 --> 00:04:53.164
but we have 59 job openings,

00:04:53.164 --> 00:04:55.114
and I expect this to be 450

00:04:55.114 --> 00:04:57.109
by the time the far majority of people

00:04:57.109 --> 00:04:59.712
watch this episode or listen to it, right?

00:04:59.712 --> 00:05:00.964
So,

00:05:00.964 --> 00:05:02.450
I guess that's where I'm willing to go.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.309
I'm not willing to say we're 7,000.

00:05:04.309 --> 00:05:05.935
I'm not willing to say we're 1,000.

00:05:05.935 --> 00:05:08.257
I think fake it 'til you make it

00:05:08.257 --> 00:05:10.393
is a very scary line

00:05:10.393 --> 00:05:12.343
that I think most people struggle with,

00:05:13.686 --> 00:05:14.615
but you know,

00:05:14.615 --> 00:05:15.776
we live in a transparent world.

00:05:15.776 --> 00:05:16.844
Like,

00:05:16.844 --> 00:05:18.142
the reason I even say this answer

00:05:18.142 --> 00:05:20.650
is 'cause I'm always trying to correct

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:22.136
myself,

00:05:22.136 --> 00:05:24.228
'cause I'm scared to, like,

00:05:24.228 --> 00:05:25.714
get called out for,

00:05:25.714 --> 00:05:27.154
you're losing all your credibility

00:05:27.154 --> 00:05:28.638
by getting called out on something.

00:05:28.638 --> 00:05:30.356
So, it's a very fine line.

00:05:30.356 --> 00:05:32.866
I think the intent matters.

00:05:32.866 --> 00:05:34.398
For me, I'm just going in speed,

00:05:34.398 --> 00:05:36.395
rounding out numbers, I'm going fast.

00:05:36.395 --> 00:05:37.556
Not trying to trick you to think

00:05:37.556 --> 00:05:39.413
we're bigger than we are.

00:05:39.413 --> 00:05:40.805
So I would answer you a couple things.

00:05:40.805 --> 00:05:42.014
One,

00:05:42.014 --> 00:05:44.197
white lies that become truths

00:05:44.197 --> 00:05:46.052
within a very quick or
short period of time

00:05:46.052 --> 00:05:47.540
feel a little more comfortable,

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:50.698
and the intent of the white lie.

00:05:51.914 --> 00:05:53.584
If you feel like,

00:05:53.584 --> 00:05:56.093
if you feel like you're not
trying to do the wrong thing,

00:05:56.093 --> 00:05:58.322
that you're gonna be able to
deliver for the other person,

00:05:58.322 --> 00:06:00.527
you just wanna tip them over

00:06:00.527 --> 00:06:01.316
in their own,

00:06:01.316 --> 00:06:03.220
you know it's so funny, with my clients,

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:06.332
I think they get so much more benefit

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:08.468
working with us than the alternative

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:09.445
that I'm like,

00:06:09.445 --> 00:06:10.790
I feel like I'm helping them along.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:12.882
I feel like it's in their
best interest, not mine.

00:06:12.882 --> 00:06:15.016
Of course there's a secondary
best interest of mine,

00:06:15.016 --> 00:06:15.991
and it's funny,

00:06:15.991 --> 00:06:17.616
I tend to not,

00:06:17.616 --> 00:06:19.377
you all right?

00:06:19.377 --> 00:06:21.141
What's that?

00:06:21.141 --> 00:06:23.015
(laughs)

00:06:24.439 --> 00:06:25.785
Are we meeting?
- Yes.

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:26.851
- I'm running just a few minutes late.

00:06:26.851 --> 00:06:28.616
Do you wanna say hi to the VaynerNation?

00:06:28.616 --> 00:06:30.494
- Hi guys! I'm Claire.

00:06:30.494 --> 00:06:31.429
- Um,

00:06:33.513 --> 00:06:35.680
you know, I think that it's all about

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.862
like every other answer which is,

00:06:37.862 --> 00:06:39.207
it needs to have the right intent

00:06:39.207 --> 00:06:40.554
and it needs to be appropriate.

00:06:40.554 --> 00:06:42.166
So, that's what I got.

00:06:42.166 --> 00:06:42.667
- Hey Gary.

00:06:42.667 --> 00:06:45.641
Miles Keever with HappyHumanoids.com.

00:06:45.641 --> 00:06:47.822
In episode 49, you were asked a question

00:06:47.822 --> 00:06:50.887
about high-end wine
business and was it a hoax.

00:06:50.887 --> 00:06:53.859
Now, I thought your answer
was brilliant and profound.

00:06:53.859 --> 00:06:57.111
So profound that I checked
out and I began observing

00:06:57.111 --> 00:06:59.155
your staff members behind you.

00:06:59.155 --> 00:07:01.846
Lots of them are standing without chairs,

00:07:01.846 --> 00:07:04.402
and when you asked Lou to go get DeMayo,

00:07:04.402 --> 00:07:07.140
somebody snatched his
chair out of the way.

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.371
Do you have a set up in your community?

00:07:09.371 --> 00:07:12.111
A way to keep the competitive edge going

00:07:12.111 --> 00:07:14.340
by not having enough chairs?

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.476
- First of all, who's Lou?

00:07:16.476 --> 00:07:17.482
- [Man] Little Lou!

00:07:17.482 --> 00:07:18.503
- Oh, Little Lou!

00:07:18.503 --> 00:07:19.804
You're right.

00:07:19.804 --> 00:07:21.549
I literally think of him as Little Lou.

00:07:23.269 --> 00:07:24.853
This question

00:07:24.853 --> 00:07:27.590
is so perfect for episode 50

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:30.609
because I'm so

00:07:30.609 --> 00:07:33.444
devastated that I'm not

00:07:33.444 --> 00:07:35.067
limiting chairs on purpose

00:07:35.067 --> 00:07:37.716
to create a competitive culture,

00:07:37.716 --> 00:07:39.943
so the answer is no.

00:07:39.943 --> 00:07:42.265
I haven't created that on purpose,

00:07:42.265 --> 00:07:46.073
but yes, yes, yes, do I wish I did.

00:07:46.073 --> 00:07:48.118
I love the thesis behind it.

00:07:48.118 --> 00:07:52.437
I think it's a phenomenal observation.

00:07:52.437 --> 00:07:55.222
I desperately wish it was true.

00:07:55.222 --> 00:07:56.661
And I will say this,

00:07:56.661 --> 00:08:00.796
I don't have any hardcore tactics

00:08:00.796 --> 00:08:02.189
to create competitiveness.

00:08:02.189 --> 00:08:02.916
And as a matter of fact,

00:08:02.916 --> 00:08:04.541
one of the biggest things that I really

00:08:04.541 --> 00:08:06.308
value is that a lot of the senior people

00:08:06.308 --> 00:08:07.653
that have been coming into VaynerMedia

00:08:07.653 --> 00:08:09.650
say this is the first
agency they'd ever worked in

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:12.067
that people are competitive
because they wanna do great work

00:08:12.067 --> 00:08:15.873
but not at the cost of their
other employees around them

00:08:15.873 --> 00:08:17.823
which means we're building real culture.

00:08:17.823 --> 00:08:18.986
It excites me

00:08:18.986 --> 00:08:20.983
and it's something that I strive for.

00:08:20.983 --> 00:08:22.885
But I do think everything
stems from the top,

00:08:22.885 --> 00:08:25.114
and I don't know how I do it,

00:08:25.114 --> 00:08:28.225
but boy, do I know I breed
competitiveness here,

00:08:28.225 --> 00:08:30.493
because I'm competitive as (bleep).

00:08:30.493 --> 00:08:32.120
- [Voiceover] Terri says,

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.022
"I saw a discussion via Linkedin

00:08:34.022 --> 00:08:36.024
"about Klout scores on a resume.

00:08:36.024 --> 00:08:38.150
"What are your thoughts on them?"

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:39.590
- Terri, this is a question.

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:41.403
I'm glad everybody's gonna
get to hear my answer

00:08:41.403 --> 00:08:43.121
and this has nothing
to do with my feelings

00:08:43.121 --> 00:08:45.118
towards Klout itself.

00:08:45.118 --> 00:08:47.765
These kind of scoring systems

00:08:47.765 --> 00:08:51.759
that try to prove your social
equity in the marketplace

00:08:51.759 --> 00:08:53.382
are not things I'm a big fan of.

00:08:53.382 --> 00:08:54.823
They're super gameable.

00:08:54.823 --> 00:08:57.951
Let me say something really interesting.

00:08:58.864 --> 00:09:00.659
Pssst.

00:09:00.659 --> 00:09:02.046
Psst.

00:09:02.046 --> 00:09:05.434
A lot of you have hit me up on Twitter

00:09:05.434 --> 00:09:07.431
because if I replied to you,

00:09:07.431 --> 00:09:09.569
you knew that your
Klout score would go up.

00:09:09.569 --> 00:09:11.517
Now this is more of a 2012 move

00:09:11.517 --> 00:09:12.818
and has become something

00:09:12.818 --> 00:09:14.443
less and less of you have done,

00:09:14.443 --> 00:09:17.324
but you know, if you look
yourself in the mirror right now,

00:09:17.324 --> 00:09:19.040
you knew that you were trying to game me

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:20.157
for more Klout scores

00:09:20.157 --> 00:09:22.154
and I was thrilled to give it to you

00:09:22.154 --> 00:09:24.569
because I know how
little it actually meant.

00:09:24.569 --> 00:09:26.099
And because I love you

00:09:26.099 --> 00:09:27.252
and that you even know who I am

00:09:27.252 --> 00:09:29.019
and I wanted to give you some love,

00:09:29.019 --> 00:09:31.527
but people who literally tweeted out,

00:09:31.527 --> 00:09:33.336
like oh, got some Klout juice,

00:09:33.336 --> 00:09:35.426
because they engaged
with me on that platform.

00:09:35.426 --> 00:09:37.283
Look, my answer is very simple,

00:09:37.283 --> 00:09:38.816
and very, very hardcore,

00:09:38.816 --> 00:09:41.277
which is, I never signed
up for a Klout account

00:09:41.277 --> 00:09:43.506
while I had one of the
bigger scores up there,

00:09:43.506 --> 00:09:45.458
so that's kind of called

00:09:45.458 --> 00:09:48.336
eating your own dog food, right?

00:09:48.336 --> 00:09:50.054
Taking your own medicine.

00:09:50.054 --> 00:09:52.006
I believe in it so little

00:09:52.006 --> 00:09:53.583
that I didn't even want to acknowledge it

00:09:53.583 --> 00:09:54.932
by signing up,

00:09:54.932 --> 00:09:56.604
even though it was probably
in my best interest

00:09:56.604 --> 00:09:58.415
both personally, and too, professionally

00:09:58.415 --> 00:10:00.131
to just understand how it worked.

00:10:00.131 --> 00:10:02.035
So again, has nothing to do with Klout.

00:10:02.035 --> 00:10:04.496
I feel the same way about
a million other things.

00:10:04.496 --> 00:10:05.657
It is not something,

00:10:05.657 --> 00:10:09.514
the big data version of your worth

00:10:09.514 --> 00:10:11.602
is not something I'm super pumped about.

00:10:11.602 --> 00:10:13.322
You obviously might have seen

00:10:13.322 --> 00:10:15.922
my own last Medium article about empathy

00:10:15.922 --> 00:10:18.476
and the hidden truths of people.

00:10:18.476 --> 00:10:20.286
I'm so much more into the

00:10:20.286 --> 00:10:23.769
grey, intuitive and EQ of people

00:10:23.769 --> 00:10:25.535
than I am the black and white,

00:10:25.535 --> 00:10:26.741
which is what Klout is.

00:10:26.741 --> 00:10:28.322
It's just raw data dumped

00:10:28.322 --> 00:10:29.806
and figured, if like,

00:10:29.806 --> 00:10:32.501
it's high school math.

00:10:32.501 --> 00:10:33.801
And when I mean high school math,

00:10:33.801 --> 00:10:34.821
go deeper with me,

00:10:34.821 --> 00:10:37.284
it's the math of how high school works.

00:10:37.284 --> 00:10:39.838
The popular girl decides that

00:10:39.838 --> 00:10:42.065
she likes Steve and
she goes out with him.

00:10:42.065 --> 00:10:43.877
Well, his Klout score
went through the roof

00:10:43.877 --> 00:10:45.688
because he was in the band before that.

00:10:45.688 --> 00:10:47.957
You know, so it's that kind of thing.

00:10:47.957 --> 00:10:49.954
- [Voiceover] Dan wants to know

00:10:49.954 --> 00:10:52.185
"Are there any common mistakes you repeat

00:10:52.185 --> 00:10:53.856
"over and over again?

00:10:53.856 --> 00:10:57.125
"Either way, any tips
for overcoming them?"

00:10:57.755 --> 00:10:58.870
- Dan,

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:01.751
I think I repeat all my
mistakes over and over again.

00:11:01.751 --> 00:11:03.421
First of all, my thesis on life

00:11:03.421 --> 00:11:04.862
is scaling the unscalable,

00:11:04.862 --> 00:11:07.509
which is one big mistake in itself.

00:11:07.509 --> 00:11:09.367
We just had a meeting with the whole crew

00:11:09.367 --> 00:11:11.225
about all the mistakes I'm making,

00:11:11.225 --> 00:11:12.244
bottle of,

00:11:12.244 --> 00:11:13.637
bottom of the funnel things.

00:11:13.637 --> 00:11:15.842
So you know, like,

00:11:15.842 --> 00:11:17.003
you know,

00:11:17.003 --> 00:11:19.930
I'm a big believer of betting
on strengths, not weaknesses,

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:22.020
you know, you try to get better

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:23.690
as you go through the process

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:25.640
and go along.

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:27.872
I don't know if I'm the
right person to answer this.

00:11:27.872 --> 00:11:30.054
I repeat a lot of mistakes.

00:11:30.054 --> 00:11:32.144
I continue to always,

00:11:32.144 --> 00:11:33.999
I have good moments for a year or two

00:11:33.999 --> 00:11:35.207
or a month of two of

00:11:35.207 --> 00:11:37.345
not trying to bite off
more than I can chew,

00:11:37.345 --> 00:11:38.922
but then I get right into it.

00:11:38.922 --> 00:11:40.919
For all the process and scalability,

00:11:40.919 --> 00:11:42.268
I continue to create things

00:11:42.268 --> 00:11:43.986
that allow me to be the bottleneck,

00:11:43.986 --> 00:11:45.276
though I think this crew and I

00:11:45.276 --> 00:11:46.857
are doing a good job of hacking away

00:11:46.857 --> 00:11:49.550
and we have an ambition
for '15 to be better at it

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:51.824
in my limited time world.

00:11:51.824 --> 00:11:53.449
You know, I...

00:11:53.449 --> 00:11:56.096
But, really, these are
all hard questions for me

00:11:56.096 --> 00:11:57.538
because I really

00:11:57.538 --> 00:11:59.860
struggle to think about what I'm bad at

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:03.203
because I spend all my time
thinking about what I'm great at

00:12:03.203 --> 00:12:04.920
and those are a lot of things.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:07.102
And that's where I put my energy and time

00:12:07.102 --> 00:12:09.842
and that's why I think
I get the permission

00:12:09.842 --> 00:12:11.516
and the luxury

00:12:11.516 --> 00:12:13.783
and the awesome feeling
of having so many of you

00:12:13.783 --> 00:12:15.453
watch this show,

00:12:15.453 --> 00:12:19.124
betting on strengths is
the grossly underestimated

00:12:19.124 --> 00:12:21.167
execution of our time.

00:12:21.167 --> 00:12:23.814
And that's what I do and I do it well,

00:12:23.814 --> 00:12:27.204
and so I answered it out of
being a cordial human being,

00:12:27.204 --> 00:12:28.456
but the truth is,

00:12:28.456 --> 00:12:30.827
I don't give a rat's
ass about my weaknesses.

00:12:30.827 --> 00:12:33.706
I surely don't know how to fix them

00:12:33.706 --> 00:12:35.608
because

00:12:35.608 --> 00:12:37.140
I don't focus on them much.

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:38.301
Once in a while,

00:12:38.301 --> 00:12:40.252
and that's that.

00:12:40.252 --> 00:12:41.972
And so in final thoughts,

00:12:41.972 --> 00:12:44.849
I want to thank so many of you

00:12:44.849 --> 00:12:48.611
for watching the first 50
episodes of the #AskGaryVee Show.

00:12:49.754 --> 00:12:51.098
I don't know how many more of these

00:12:51.098 --> 00:12:52.259
I have in tank,

00:12:52.259 --> 00:12:54.815
but it's been an amazing ride.

00:12:54.815 --> 00:12:55.930
Don't worry, DRock.

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:57.509
I just mentioned we're
gonna do episode 100.

